From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 21:24:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: decoder mode: decode the hwaccel on the decode
 thread

Decoder mode decoded HEVC on rpi-hevc-dec (the v4l2request hwaccel) on
the main loop, and only a codec without the hwaccel (H.264) on the
decode thread (q11-074). Each 4K frame's decode held the main loop while
the hardware worked, so DomA's virtqueue kicks and display commands
waited behind it: DomA saw the frames come out in bursts with gaps of
28-170 ms, and a WebView playing a 4K60 stream decoded 57 of them a
second, dropped 15 and showed 42. The device model's display commands
themselves were quick: with QEMU_LAT_DIAG, still on the main loop,
every RESOURCE_FLUSH and SET_SCANOUT was answered within 2 ms.

Every codec is decoded on the thread now:
  - The hwaccel keeps one libavcodec thread, as on the main loop (frame
    threads with it were not tried), and when the hwaccel's DRM device
    is open the thread keeps at most 2 frames decoded ahead instead of
    4: a hardware frame waiting there holds a pool buffer the display
    could use. An HEVC profile the hwaccel does not take is decoded in
    software on the thread too, with the same bound.
  - The display path reads the decoder's reordering depth from the
    thread's last view (thr_reorder) instead of the AVCodecContext the
    thread owns. The main loop's branches for a codec without the
    thread go, and the comments that assumed one follow.
  - Stopping the input, a START that ends a drain, switching the codec
    and closing first let go of what holds pool buffers on this side
    (the display's references to frames not on the display, the frame
    held across a format change, the frames the GPU conversion holds),
    then wait for the thread, which may be waiting for a free pool
    buffer. A frame on the display stays referenced until weston
    releases it, which only the main loop hears, so such a wait ends at
    rpidistro-ffmpeg 0002's bound (300 ms).

The bound on the frames kept for the display (ds_refs_cap, q11-071) was
the references held when the pool came near its end plus one. On the
thread the decoder runs ahead of the display at the start of a stream:
in one of two runs that came out at 12 and a 4K60 WebView showed 41.5
frames a second (56.9 in the other). The bound is now at least
DEC_POOL_MAX - 2 less what the decoder holds on its side (the
reordering depth and the frames waiting on the thread), 26 with a
reordering depth of 2, the value the main loop gave for 1080p60, and
it is set two buffers earlier, as those frames are not seen yet either.

Measured on a 16 GB Raspberry Pi 5 running the 8g image, DomD's CMA
640 MiB, DomA's scanout buffers linear, with q11-081/082 (2026-10-19),
on a 60 s HEVC clip measured for 30 s with nothing else polling the
board. (The 15 s test clip's loop restarts the decoder: on the thread
it showed 48 frames a second where the 60 s clip showed 58. Polling
DomA's processes over adb meanwhile took a run down to 27-28.)
  - 4K60 full screen in a WebView: 42.1 frames shown a second on the
    main loop. With this as it is and SurfaceFlinger's SF phase at 8 ms
    (the rpi5 product): 57.0 and 56.9, and 56.5 and 57.7 among the HMI.
    A build before q11-081's last change gave 56.1 and 53.0 full screen
    and 55.2 and 51.4 among the HMI without the phase, 56.6 and 52.7,
    57.4 and 57.9 with it; one before the last changes here gave
    53.2-58.1 full screen with the phase in five runs.
  - 1080p60 among the HMI: 59.7 (the build before the last changes).
  - codec -T 60.7 fps for the 4K stream and for H.264 1080p60
    (software); -S (decode alone) of the 4K stream 67.7 -> 69.4 fps
    with 4 frames ahead and the old bound.
  - In the fourteen runs of these two builds the bound was set in six,
    at 26 (and in two of the six stop tries); in the others the pool did
    not reach 26 buffers and none was set. 1659-1970 conversions were
    skipped in each 4K run, none was lost, no pool wait was given up,
    hvs_underrun stayed 0 and the HDMI audio's position moved (once it
    went back, the stream restarting). CmaFree's lowest during 4K was
    23-111 MB; the build before the last changes here took it to 0 kB
    and 40 kB in two runs, with nothing lost and no allocation failure
    in qemu's log.
  - Stopping the app during 4K playback returned in 172-297 ms in six
    tries, and the next playback showed 56.7 and 57.4 frames a
    second.
  - Not run: two decoders at once (the case the flush rule of q11-081
    is for).

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c | 173 +++++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 109 insertions(+), 64 deletions(-)

diff --git a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
index 986311b..e7fcc05 100644
--- a/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
+++ b/hw/virtio/virtio-media-decoder.c
@@ -48,9 +48,9 @@
  * ONE THING DECODER MODE GETS FOR FREE. The proxy path has to poll a host fd
  * for events, which is where the vb2_poll()/file-I/O-emulation trap lives (see
  * virtio_media_update_fd_handler). Here there is no host fd and no poll:
- * completions and SOURCE_CHANGE are produced by the QBUF that caused them, or,
- * for a codec decoded on its own thread (q11-dec-thread), by a bottom half when
- * frames come out of it, so that entire failure mode does not exist.
+ * completions and SOURCE_CHANGE are produced by the QBUF that caused them, or
+ * by a bottom half when frames come out of the decode thread (q11-dec-thread),
+ * so that entire failure mode does not exist.
  *
  * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
  */
@@ -200,7 +200,8 @@ typedef struct DecGpuEntry {
  * (dec_ds_keep says which), and that frame is shown from the guest's copy
  * instead. 384 MiB lets the pool's bound decide (ds_refs_cap; for frames of
  * 12 MB or less it is the tighter one -- a 4K frame is 12.4 MB, and the
- * Raspberry Pi 5 kept 24 of them): a WebView playing a 4K60 stream
+ * Raspberry Pi 5 kept 24 of them, 26 on the decode thread with
+ * q11-dec-hw-thread's bound): a WebView playing a 4K60 stream
  * shows a frame some 21-24 frames after its decode, and with 256 MiB (21 frames)
  * it showed 25.6-42.4 frames a second, swinging between runs -- each frame
  * shown after its reference went needs the late conversion, which delays the
@@ -228,19 +229,24 @@ typedef struct DecGpuEntry {
 /*
  * libavcodec's pool is rpi-hevc-dec's CAPTURE queue, which takes VB2_MAX_FRAME
  * (32) buffers and no more. Past that v4l2request waits for a buffer to come
- * free, and only this thread frees them: with every buffer referenced here
- * that wait would never end (rpidistro-ffmpeg 0002 bounds it, dec_ds_starved).
+ * free, on the decode thread, and only the main loop letting a reference here
+ * go can end that wait: with every buffer referenced here it lasts until
+ * rpidistro-ffmpeg 0002's bound (dec_ds_starved).
  * The pool grows only when no buffer is free, and a buffer is seen here only
- * when its frame comes out, up to has_b_frames frames after it was taken. So
- * when the import of the (DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT - has_b_frames)-th buffer appears,
- * every buffer of the DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT is in use, and the references held then
- * plus one more is how many the pool has room for: they are not let grow past
- * that (ds_refs_cap) for as long as the pool lives, which leaves the last
- * buffer for the decoder's own references varying. Should the decoder run out
- * even so (dec_ds_starved), the bound comes down by two. (The Raspberry Pi 5,
- * HEVC 1080p60 with has_b_frames 2: set at the 28th import, 26 frames. With
- * the held references alone, 23 or 24 at that point, a bound of 24 let frames
- * the compositor still held go, 25 did not.)
+ * when its frame comes out, up to has_b_frames frames after it was taken, and
+ * thr_out_max more on the decode thread. So when the import of the
+ * (DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT - has_b_frames - thr_out_max)-th buffer appears,
+ * the pool is nearly full, and the references are not let grow past
+ * DEC_POOL_MAX - 2 less what the decoder holds on its side (has_b_frames, and
+ * the frames waiting on the decode thread, thr_out_max), or the references
+ * held then plus one more if that is higher (ds_refs_cap), for as long as the
+ * pool lives. Should the decoder run out even so (dec_ds_starved), the bound
+ * comes down by two. (The Raspberry Pi 5, HEVC 1080p60 with has_b_frames 2:
+ * 26 frames. A bound of 24 let frames the compositor still held go, 25 did
+ * not. Taken from the references held alone, the bound came out at 12 in one
+ * of two runs once HEVC was decoded on the thread, which runs ahead of the
+ * display at the start of a stream; a 4K60 WebView then showed 41.5 frames a
+ * second, against 56.9 in the other run.)
  */
 #define DEC_POOL_MAX 32
 #define DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT (DEC_POOL_MAX - 2)
@@ -316,18 +322,27 @@ struct DecZcPool {
 };
 
 /*
- * q11-dec-thread: a codec without the hwaccel (H.264) is decoded on a thread
- * of its own; HEVC stays on the main loop, also when it falls back to
- * software. On the main loop H.264 1080p60 took 96 to 98% of a core on the
- * Raspberry Pi 5, with DomA's virtio-blk served by the same loop; with the
- * thread the main loop takes 28 to 29% and the thread 72 to 73%.
+ * q11-dec-thread: every codec is decoded on a thread of its own. On the main
+ * loop H.264 1080p60 took 96 to 98% of a core on the Raspberry Pi 5, with
+ * DomA's virtio-blk served by the same loop; with the thread the main loop
+ * takes 28 to 29% and the thread 72 to 73%. q11-dec-hw-thread: the hwaccel
+ * (HEVC on rpi-hevc-dec) is there too. On the main loop each 4K frame's
+ * decode held the loop while the hardware worked, so the guest's virtqueue
+ * kicks and display commands waited behind it and frames came out in bursts
+ * with gaps of 28-170 ms; a WebView playing 4K60 decoded 57 of them a second,
+ * dropped 15 and showed 42. On the thread it decoded 57.6-60.3 and showed
+ * 51.4-57.9 a second, full screen and among the HMI (Raspberry Pi 5,
+ * 2026-10-19).
  * The thread owns avctx; the main loop hands it packets and takes decoded
  * frames (dec_av_send / dec_av_receive / dec_av_flush), and everything else --
  * the guest's buffers, events, GL -- stays on the main loop. Bounded both
- * ways: DEC_THR_IN packets waiting, DEC_THR_OUT frames decoded ahead.
+ * ways: DEC_THR_IN packets waiting, thr_out_max frames decoded ahead
+ * (DEC_THR_OUT; 2 with the hwaccel's DRM device open).
  */
 #define DEC_THR_IN  DEC_MAX_BACKLOG_PKTS_THR
 #define DEC_THR_OUT 4
+/* dec_ds_keep's bound stays above zero with the deepest reordering it allows (16) */
+QEMU_BUILD_BUG_ON(DEC_THR_OUT + 16 >= DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT);
 
 typedef struct DecThrOut {
     AVFrame *frame;   /* NULL: err below */
@@ -519,6 +534,8 @@ struct VirtIOMediaDec {
     GQueue *thr_out;        /* DecThrOut * */
     bool thr_quit, thr_flush, thr_eof;
     QEMUBH *thr_bh;
+    int thr_reorder;        /* avctx->has_b_frames after the last frame (thr_lock) */
+    unsigned thr_out_max;   /* decoded ahead at most (2 with the hwaccel's device) */
 };
 
 static AVPacket dec_thr_eos;   /* the drain request in thr_in */
@@ -1007,7 +1024,8 @@ static bool dec_ds_keep(VirtIOMediaDec *d, unsigned slot, unsigned index,
 {
     DecGpuEntry *e = &d->gpu[slot];
     unsigned max = dec_ds_max_refs(bytes);
-    unsigned soft = DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT - (unsigned)MIN(MAX(reorder, 0), 16);
+    unsigned soft = DEC_DS_POOL_SOFT - (unsigned)MIN(MAX(reorder, 0), 16) -
+                    d->thr_out_max;
 
     /*
      * Normally a no-op: the decoder could write fr into this buffer only once
@@ -1018,10 +1036,13 @@ static bool dec_ds_keep(VirtIOMediaDec *d, unsigned slot, unsigned index,
     dec_ds_unref(d, slot);
     if (!d->ds_refs_cap && d->gpu_n >= soft) {
         /* fr, in the buffer that just appeared, is one of them; one more */
-        d->ds_refs_cap = d->ds_refs + 2;
+        d->ds_refs_cap = MAX(d->ds_refs + 2,
+                             DEC_POOL_MAX - 2 - (unsigned)MIN(MAX(reorder, 0), 16) -
+                             d->thr_out_max);
         info_report("virtio-media/dec: sand-scanout: %u of the decoder's "
-                    "buffers seen, up to %d more decoded ahead: keeping %u "
-                    "frames at most", d->gpu_n, reorder, d->ds_refs_cap);
+                    "buffers seen, %d held for reordering and up to %u decoded "
+                    "ahead: keeping %u frames at most", d->gpu_n, reorder,
+                    d->thr_out_max, d->ds_refs_cap);
     }
     if (d->ds_refs_cap) {
         /* near the end of the pool: replace one, do not add */
@@ -1092,6 +1113,21 @@ static void dec_ds_release(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * Before waiting for the decode thread, which may wait for a free pool buffer:
+ * let go of what holds pool buffers on this side. A frame on the display stays
+ * referenced until the display lets it go, which only the main loop hears, so a
+ * wait for one ends at rpidistro-ffmpeg 0002's bound (300 ms).
+ */
+static void dec_let_go(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
+{
+    dec_ds_release(d);
+    av_frame_free(&d->held);
+    while (d->gpu_held_n) {
+        dec_gpu_unhold_oldest(d);
+    }
+}
+
 /* Offer the decoder's buffers now? See DEC_DS_PROBE_MS. */
 static bool dec_ds_wanted(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
 {
@@ -1159,6 +1195,17 @@ static bool dec_ds_catch_up(void *owner)
     return !d->ds_pend_n;
 }
 
+/* frames libavcodec holds back for reordering (the decode thread's last view) */
+static int dec_reorder(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
+{
+    int r;
+
+    qemu_mutex_lock(&d->thr_lock);
+    r = d->thr_reorder;
+    qemu_mutex_unlock(&d->thr_lock);
+    return r;
+}
+
 /* Every entry goes (dec_gpu_clear): the display forgets this owner's buffers. */
 static void dec_ds_drop(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
 {
@@ -1439,7 +1486,6 @@ static int dec_gpu_write(VirtIOMediaSession *s, VirtIOMediaBuffer *gb,
     }
 
     /* q11-dec-sand-scanout: offer the decoder's buffer to the display */
-    assert(!d->thr_on);   /* avctx below: a hwaccel codec has no decode thread */
     tex = virtio_media_gpu_res_tex(gb->virgl_res);
     /* q11-dec-sand-scaled: a scaled frame too, at its size (the display scales) */
     show = (!scaled || dec_ds_scaled_on()) && tex && dec_ds_on() &&
@@ -1450,7 +1496,7 @@ static int dec_gpu_write(VirtIOMediaSession *s, VirtIOMediaBuffer *gb,
     show = show && e->show_fd >= 0 &&
            dec_ds_keep(d, (unsigned)(e - d->gpu),
                        (unsigned)(gb - s->queues[1].guest), fr,
-                       desc->objects[0].size, d->avctx->has_b_frames);
+                       desc->objects[0].size, dec_reorder(d));
     skip = show && dec_ds_skip_on() && direct_scanout_skip_copy(d);
 
     if (!skip) {
@@ -2456,7 +2502,7 @@ static void *dec_thr_run(void *opaque)
             qemu_cond_broadcast(&d->thr_cond);
             continue;
         }
-        while (!d->thr_eof && g_queue_get_length(d->thr_out) < DEC_THR_OUT) {
+        while (!d->thr_eof && g_queue_get_length(d->thr_out) < d->thr_out_max) {
             if (!f) {
                 f = av_frame_alloc();
                 if (!f) {
@@ -2469,6 +2515,7 @@ static void *dec_thr_run(void *opaque)
                 dec_zc_done(d, f);
             }
             qemu_mutex_lock(&d->thr_lock);
+            d->thr_reorder = d->avctx->has_b_frames;
             if (d->thr_flush || d->thr_quit) {
                 av_frame_unref(f);
                 break;
@@ -2552,6 +2599,12 @@ static void dec_thr_start(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
 
     d->thr_in = g_queue_new();
     d->thr_out = g_queue_new();
+    /*
+     * With the hwaccel's DRM device open (an HEVC profile it does not take is
+     * decoded in software, on this thread too, and gets the same bound): a
+     * hardware frame waiting here holds a pool buffer the display could use.
+     */
+    d->thr_out_max = d->codec->hwaccel && d->hwdev ? 2 : DEC_THR_OUT;
     d->thr_quit = d->thr_flush = d->thr_eof = false;
     qemu_mutex_init(&d->thr_lock);
     qemu_cond_init(&d->thr_cond);
@@ -2561,7 +2614,11 @@ static void dec_thr_start(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
     d->thr_on = true;
 }
 
-/* Waits for the packet being decoded, at most one frame's decode. */
+/*
+ * Waits for the packet being decoded: one frame's decode, or, for a hardware
+ * decode waiting for a free pool buffer, until rpidistro-ffmpeg 0002's bound
+ * (the callers let the display's references go first).
+ */
 static void dec_thr_stop(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
 {
     if (!d->thr_on) {
@@ -2592,9 +2649,6 @@ static int dec_av_send(VirtIOMediaDec *d, const AVPacket *pkt)
     if (!d->avctx) {
         return AVERROR(ENODEV);
     }
-    if (!d->thr_on) {
-        return avcodec_send_packet(d->avctx, pkt);
-    }
     qemu_mutex_lock(&d->thr_lock);
     /* the drain request is never refused: its callers cannot resend it */
     if (pkt && g_queue_get_length(d->thr_in) >= DEC_THR_IN) {
@@ -2621,9 +2675,6 @@ static int dec_av_receive(VirtIOMediaDec *d, AVFrame *frame)
     if (!d->avctx) {
         return AVERROR(ENODEV);
     }
-    if (!d->thr_on) {
-        return avcodec_receive_frame(d->avctx, frame);
-    }
     qemu_mutex_lock(&d->thr_lock);
     o = g_queue_peek_head(d->thr_out);
     if (!o) {
@@ -2652,10 +2703,6 @@ static void dec_av_flush(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
     if (!d->avctx) {
         return;
     }
-    if (!d->thr_on) {
-        avcodec_flush_buffers(d->avctx);
-        return;
-    }
     qemu_mutex_lock(&d->thr_lock);
     g_queue_clear_full(d->thr_in, dec_thr_in_free);
     d->thr_flush = true;
@@ -2669,8 +2716,9 @@ static void dec_av_flush(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
 /*
  * THE INPUT BACKLOG EXISTS BECAUSE VIDIOC_QBUF MUST NOT FAIL.
  *
- * Decoding happens inside the OUTPUT QBUF that carries the packet, so
- * without a backlog libavcodec being full surfaces as QBUF returning -EAGAIN.
+ * The OUTPUT QBUF that carries the packet hands it to the decode thread, so
+ * without a backlog the thread's queue being full surfaces as QBUF returning
+ * -EAGAIN.
  * No V4L2 client expects that. v4l2_codec2 fails the decode outright
  * (pumpDecodeRequest -> Failed to QBUF to input queue -> state Error); on the
  * board a 1080p HEVC stream died that way on its fourth input buffer, while
@@ -2683,8 +2731,7 @@ static void dec_av_flush(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
  * So take the bytes and hold them. A real driver does the same -- the OUTPUT
  * buffer completes once its data has been copied out, long before any frame
  * comes back -- and the backlog is drained from dec_pump(), i.e. from the next
- * QBUF on either queue, or for a codec on the decode thread from dec_thr_bh,
- * once there is room.
+ * QBUF on either queue, or from dec_thr_bh, once there is room.
  *
  * ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT. Everything that feeds libavcodec has to go through
  * the queue once the queue is non-empty; see dec_qbuf.
@@ -2779,8 +2826,8 @@ static void dec_backlog_drain(VirtIOMediaDec *d)
  *
  * Called from every path that can change either side of that condition: an
  * OUTPUT QBUF (new input), a CAPTURE QBUF (new space) and DECODER_CMD(STOP)
- * (end of input), and, for a codec decoded on the thread (q11-dec-thread),
- * dec_thr_bh when frames come out of it.
+ * (end of input), and dec_thr_bh when frames come out of the decode thread
+ * (q11-dec-thread).
  */
 static void dec_pump(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
 {
@@ -2813,12 +2860,12 @@ static void dec_pump(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
     /*
      * BOUNDED. This runs on the QEMU main loop from a virtqueue handler, and the
      * guest can have 32 CAPTURE buffers queued, so an unbounded loop let one
-     * OUTPUT QBUF perform 32 software decodes plus 32 colour conversions plus 32
-     * SG copies before the main loop turned again -- at 4K that is hundreds of
-     * megabytes of memory traffic, during which DomA's virtio-blk (served by
-     * this same QEMU) is stopped. Bounding it per call costs nothing in
-     * throughput because the next QBUF pumps again. (A codec on the decode
-     * thread decodes there; this loop only takes its frames.)
+     * OUTPUT QBUF perform 32 decodes (when they were made here) plus 32 colour
+     * conversions plus 32 SG copies before the main loop turned again -- at 4K
+     * that is hundreds of megabytes of memory traffic, during which DomA's
+     * virtio-blk (served by this same QEMU) is stopped. Bounding it per call
+     * costs nothing in throughput because the next QBUF pumps again. (The
+     * decode thread decodes; this loop only takes its frames.)
      */
     for (n = 0; n < DEC_PUMP_MAX_FRAMES; n++) {
         int r;
@@ -3126,12 +3173,11 @@ static int dec_codec_open(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
         return ENOMEM;
     }
     /*
-     * One libavcodec thread for a codec decoded on the main loop (HEVC):
-     * completions there are produced by the QBUF that caused them, and frame
-     * threads would make libavcodec hold frames across those calls.
+     * One libavcodec thread for the hwaccel (HEVC), as when it was decoded on
+     * the main loop (frame threads with the hwaccel were not tried).
      *
-     * q11-dec-frame-threads: a codec on the decode thread (H.264) gets two
-     * frame threads. Its frames already come out of that thread in decode
+     * q11-dec-frame-threads: a software codec (H.264) gets two frame
+     * threads. Its frames already come out of that thread in decode
      * order and are completed by the bottom half, so the one frame libavcodec
      * holds back is only latency. A 1080p60 H.264 stream played in a WebView
      * (High, one slice a picture, so slice threads would not help) needs about
@@ -3176,10 +3222,8 @@ static int dec_codec_open(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
         avcodec_free_context(&d->avctx);
         return ENOMEM;
     }
-    /* q11-dec-thread: a codec without the hwaccel decodes off the main loop */
-    if (!d->codec->hwaccel) {
-        dec_thr_start(s);
-    }
+    /* q11-dec-thread: decoded off the main loop */
+    dec_thr_start(s);
     return 0;
 }
 
@@ -3196,12 +3240,12 @@ static int dec_codec_switch(VirtIOMediaSession *s, const DecCodec *c)
         s->queues[1].streaming) {
         return EBUSY;
     }
+    dec_let_go(d);   /* a decode waiting for a pool buffer gets one */
     dec_thr_stop(d);
     dec_gpu_reset(d);
     dec_sw_clear(d);
     dec_zc_gpu_clear(d);
     dec_backlog_clear(d);
-    av_frame_free(&d->held);
     avcodec_free_context(&d->avctx);
     dec_zc_drop_pool(d);
     qatomic_set(&d->zc_off, false);
@@ -3281,6 +3325,7 @@ void virtio_media_dec_close(VirtIOMediaSession *s)
     if (!d) {
         return;
     }
+    dec_let_go(d);   /* a decode waiting for a pool buffer gets one */
     dec_thr_stop(d);
     dec_gpu_clear(d);
     dec_sw_clear(d);
@@ -3790,8 +3835,8 @@ static int dec_streamon(VirtIOMediaSession *s, uint32_t type, bool on)
         if (qi == 0) {
             /* Input stopped: the decoder's state is no longer meaningful. */
             d->failed = 0;   /* q11-dec-fail: a new start */
+            dec_let_go(d);   /* a decode waiting for a pool buffer gets one */
             dec_av_flush(d);
-            av_frame_free(&d->held);
             dec_backlog_clear(d);
             dec_gpu_reset(d);
             dec_sw_clear(d);
@@ -3866,8 +3911,8 @@ static int dec_decoder_cmd(VirtIOMediaSession *s, struct v4l2_decoder_cmd *cmd,
          * corruption from the next frame on.
